/** YD001 quality-pass translations siman 102 */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `And it was mixed. The Taz wrote: and if that mixture was mixed again in a second mixture, he wrote below siman 110 seif 8 that it is permitted. The Shach wrote that even in two mixtures it is forbidden in something that has a permitted aspect.`,
    '1#ב': `In what is not its kind. The Shach wrote: it appears that here we follow the name and not the taste — if it is equal in name, even though not equal in taste, it is its kind and is not nullified; and if it is not equal in name, even though equal in taste, it is not its kind and is nullified.`,
    '1#ג': `To whiten it. Since it is for appearance it is not nullified, as stated in Orach Chayim siman 513 seif 3 — see there. And when placing it in a pot to fix — Maharai's reason: everything placed one in another to fix is called its kind, like water and salt in dough, which is considered its kind since they fix the dough. The Taz wrote: one should not err from this regarding what we say in many places that one against two is nullified in its kind from the Torah, and not in its kind requires sixty from the Torah — to say that there too it is its kind when it fixes the pot as here, for there the law depends on taste since taste in not its kind, and this taste in the pot automatically has a stringent name as not its kind.`,
    '2#א': `In the future. Such as an egg laid on Yom Tov that will certainly be permitted after Yom Tov — unlike a doubtful tereifah egg cited afterward.`,
    '2#ב': `Doubt. Maharam Mintz challenged: why did the Mechaber write "because the permitting matter is not certain" — this law of doubtful tereifah does not apply to something that has a permitted aspect at all; for regarding an egg laid on Yom Tov it is properly called something that has a permitted aspect, for now it is forbidden and tomorrow it will be permitted — unlike a doubtful tereifah egg: even if permission is in his hand or will certainly come, nevertheless if mixed with others it should be nullified — if the chicken is tereifah the egg should be nullified; if kosher, the egg was never forbidden and something that has a permitted aspect applies only if now forbidden and later permitted. It appears the Mechaber deals with a doubtful tereifah egg also laid on Yom Tov — even if the chicken is later kosher, today the egg is forbidden because it was laid on Yom Tov; one might think if that egg mixed on Yom Tov it would not be nullified in a thousand lest the chicken be kosher and there is no prohibition — it teaches that since the permitting matter is not certain to come, perhaps it is tereifah, it is not called something that has a permitted aspect and is nullified — investigate.`,
    '3#_': `Outlay. The Shach wrote that Maharil disagrees, for kashering outlay is a small matter; proof from maaser sheni that is something that has a permitted aspect through bringing up to Jerusalem, etc. — see there. One should be concerned for his words where there is no great loss; and it appears one should wait until the vessels are not ben yomo, then they are permitted from the Torah, but each alone is forbidden to use until after twelve months — for the Tur did not write that it is not something that has a permitted aspect only because outlay is required; when there is Torah permission without any outlay, all forbid. Ben Lev ruled a dairy bowl mixed with meat bowls is not nullified in the majority since not in its kind — all forbidden; the Bach seized on this and was stringent to remove one vessel as a mere stringency like Maharam with meat pieces — but all poskim agreed in siman 109 that one need not remove even a piece; Maharam did so because he was righteous and feared eating prohibition disgracefully, etc. — not applicable to vessels. Nevertheless, without loss, be stringent like the Bach; see siman 69 seif 10. The Taz also ruled one may kasher all vessels — see there.`,
    '4#א': `Its taste. The Taz explained: if the prohibition was removed and only taste was emitted, that taste is not stringent to forbid on account of something that has a permitted aspect. And it is not like seif 1 placing an egg in a pot to fix — there the egg is for the taste of the dish — so Beit Yosef in Orach Chayim siman 513. However the Shach disagrees and wrote that for practical law it appears even if the whole taste was emitted it is not nullified; and Bach wrote similarly.`,
    '4#ב': `Itself. The Shach wrote: all poskim seized on the Rav in this, for something that has a permitted aspect — what difference if absorbed or not, ultimately there is permission for its prohibition.`,
    '4#ג': `Some say. Meaning: and the main opinion is not so — it is not called something that has a permitted aspect since for roasting it was never forbidden, as stated in siman 69.`,
    '4#ד': `The absorbed. The Taz challenged: why did the Rama need a reason about vessels because of outlay, and not say the reason is absorbed prohibition? Answer: for vessels it is also because of absorption, but the Rama wrote outlay to teach elsewhere where outlay is required it is not something that has a permitted aspect. What is different from wine that fell in a barrel of one who vowed — that is something that has a permitted aspect, for there it is not absorption but mixture and the substance of the prohibition is there? Nevertheless in the main law he agrees with the Shach that there is no distinction for something that has a permitted aspect between absorbed taste or substance — none are nullified. But what he wrote that an unsalted piece is also something that has a permitted aspect is not as he wrote in s.k. 9.`,
    '4#ה': `Before. The Shach explained: e.g. a barrel full of grapes in clusters with much clear wine before Shabbat — one may take wine on Shabbat even though more drips constantly, for it is nullified in what already exited and there is no something that has a permitted aspect since wine was never recognizable by itself; likewise a barrel of whole grapes filled with water for tamad — grapes burst later and all wine exits — one may draw on Shabbat to drink, for the little wine exiting on Shabbat mixes and nullifies in existing wine that was never once recognizable alone — and similarly — end of his words.`,
    '4#ו': `Disagree. The Shach in Torat Chatat ruled like the first reason; Maharshal disagreed. The two reasons the Mechaber brought in Orach Chayim siman 447 — one can say they also disagree whether chametz is something that has a permitted aspect.`,
    '4#ז': `One who cooks. And as explained in Orach Chayim siman 318.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Beitzah daf 3 and in several places.`,
    '2#_': `As I cited in Tur Orach Chayim siman 513.`,
    '3#_': `Tur according to Rabbeinu Tam, since he did not intend to nullify it.`,
    '4#_': `Tur in the name of Rashba in Torat HaBayit — from the law of an egg laid on Yom Tov that is permitted by evening in any case; and from the law of tevel mixed in tithed produce, that one can separate tithes from the tevel that was mixed from the tevel before him that he still intends to fix, etc.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `Whether whole, etc. Language of the Tur — meaning: not only when whole, according to the tanna of a litra of pressed figs, which otherwise would not be nullified, as stated above siman 86 seif 3; but even when beaten, and like water and salt in dough (Beitzah 39a) — there.`,
    '1#ב': `And even if there is doubt, etc. Gemara there.`,
    '1#ג': `And it was mixed, etc. Tur; and as Mordechai wrote in seif 3 of Avodah Zarah, and he derived from this that a double doubt is forbidden regarding something that has a permitted aspect; and as stated above siman 110 seif 8 in the hagahah; and Rosh in responsum part 7 of Avodah Zarah wrote that a double doubt helps; and all the more so in siman 293 seif 3 in the hagahah — only because of one doubt in the mixture — see there beginning general rule 2; and it depends on the dispute in siman 110 seif 9 in Shulchan Aruch and hagahah; and not like Rashba who wrote "and if it was mixed" refers to certainty specifically; but in Orach Chayim siman 513 stam, like the opinion of Rashba.`,
    '1#ד': `And if it was mixed, etc. As written in Nedarim 58a — everything that has a permitted aspect, such as tevel, etc.; and we say in chapter 3 of Challah that tevel forbids, etc.; and there 52a regarding one who vowed, etc. — even though it is something that has a permitted aspect; and as written there 59a; and so in Yerushalmi there; and we learn from this: this general rule was Rashba's because of R' Yehoshua — everything that has a permitted aspect, such as tevel; and what he wrote regarding hekdesh and chadash — the Sages did not give a measure except in its kind in any amount and not in its kind in sixty; and everything that has no permitted aspect, such as terumah, etc. — these vows, what do you do with them? As something that has a permitted aspect or as something that has no permitted aspect? Reasonably we act as something that has a permitted aspect, for we learned there that the elder uproots the vow from its root, etc.; and this Mishnah does for them as something that has no permitted aspect, for we learned that one who vowed from a matter and it was mixed — if there is sixty in it, it is forbidden; resolve in its kind, not in its kind; and Tosafot chapter 5 of Yevamot 38a s.v. "and let it be nullified," etc.; and one can say, etc.`,
    '1#ה': `However, etc. Tosafot there 38a s.v. "because," etc.; and one can say, etc.`,
    '1#ו': `And in Orach Chayim, etc. There seif 3 — anything for appearance, etc. — meaning: everything that comes to fix, whether for appearance or for taste, is as in its kind; but on account of appearance without something that has a permitted aspect it does not forbid; and Rashi in Zevachim 74a s.v. "and where," etc.; and even though they raised a doubt in Bava Kamma 101a whether appearance is significant and it was not resolved — poskim ruled it is a doubt, as Shulchan Aruch Choshen Mishpat end of seif 363; and there Rava distinguishes between d'oraisa and d'rabbanan — it appears that d'oraisa appearance is significant, and all the more so for R' Yochanan in Niddah 62b — one can say even d'rabbanan; and Tosafot there s.v. "from leniencies," etc.; nevertheless all this is in prohibition of benefit; and as written there "benefit that is visible," etc. — appearance helps, unlike Peri Chadash who erred here in all this.`,
    '2#א': `There are those who say, etc. Only, etc. — meaning: to exclude a doubtful tereifah egg, for even though it is possible the chicken will lay eggs or live, it is not certain; and as written below "therefore egg," etc.; and as written at the beginning of Yom Tov 3b "if not, doubtful tereifah," etc.; and even according to Rashi's explanation there — what is the challenge? It should have said "born from doubtful tereifah."`,
    '2#ב': `Or if, etc. Tosafot Bava Metzia 53a s.v. "to return"; and on this 53b s.v. "tevel," etc.: (Excerpt) Without loss. Tosafot Bava Metzia 53a s.v. "to return," etc.; but Rashi there did not explain so — see there s.v. "and they ascend," etc.; and here such as, etc.; and there is not, etc. (end).`,
    '3#_': `(Excerpt) Vessel, etc. See what is written in siman 122 seif 8 (end).`,
    '4#א': `There are those who say, etc. As above seif 2 — or, etc. — without loss.`,
    '4#ב': `That something that has a permitted aspect, etc. Like an egg and the like: (Excerpt) That something that has a permitted aspect, etc. Hagahot Alfasi chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah 69 s.v. something that has a permitted aspect, etc. (end).`,
    '4#ג': `Or that there is, etc. Like water and salt.`,
    '4#ד': `But, etc. As written at the beginning of Yom Tov 4b — this applies where it is in prohibition in existence, etc.; and therefore it was reasonable what they needed in Avodah Zarah 73b to say on tevel (in any amount) because of "like its permission," etc. — namely leaven that fell and was removed; but since Tosafot there s.v. "tevel" needed to resolve another matter, it does not appear so; and likewise in seif 3 "a vessel that became forbidden," etc.; and so too Peri Chadash.`,
    '4#ה': `And likewise if there is not, etc. All the Acharonim disagree on this — and Taz and Shach: (Excerpt) And likewise if, etc. Like a piece worthy of honor in siman 101 seif 2 (end). (Excerpt) And likewise if there is not, etc. — meaning: if the matter is not absorbed from its kind; and as Tosafot wrote there (in siman 101 s.k. 7) (end).`,
    '4#ו': `Even though some say, etc. This is the opinion of Rif in chapter 3 of Forbidden Foods who ruled "all of it" in Pesachim 76b; and even though Rav Kahana taught, etc. — that flask, etc. — that "all of it" also comes because it is something that has a permitted aspect, for it is possible to eat alone and with a Jew and a little scent — nevertheless it is so; and as written in Orach Chayim siman 447 seif 1 in the hagahah — and some are stringent, etc.; and Mordechai there in the name of Rabeinu Yehudah seized on this from that which chapter of Orlah (Yevamot chapter 2 1a) — and that which Rav Ashi, etc.; and likewise here with meat it was never forbidden; and Ran in Nedarim 52a resolved: some say for its name, since it is permitted for everyone; and above siman 108 seif 1 in the hagahah ruled not like Rif who wrote "and if one baked bread with meat," etc. — permitted in both, for this, etc.; and like the first reason Rif wrote there that "all of it" is permitted only b'dieved.`,
    '4#ז': `Every prohibition, etc. As written in Orach Chayim siman 318 seif 2: (Excerpt) Every prohibition, etc. From that which Bava Kamma seif 9 (1); and see there Tosafot s.v. "everything," etc.; and even though something that has a permitted aspect, etc.; and according to what is written here it is reasonable; and see what is written in Orach Chayim siman 320 seif 2 (end).`,
    '4#ח': `One who vowed, etc. As written in Yerushalmi chapter 6 of Nedarim and as stated above.`,
    '4#ט': `For, etc. There and in the Gemara 59a.`,
    '4#י': `Something, etc. such as, etc. From what is written in Avodah Zarah 74a — let it also teach chametz on Pesach; and if so, it would forbid anyway on account of something that has a permitted aspect; and there it excludes, etc.`,
    '4#כ': `And there are those who disagree. As Ran wrote — this is because he holds like R' Yehudah that chametz after Pesach is forbidden from the Torah; but we, who hold like R' Shimon, it is something that has a permitted aspect; and this is what Rava said in Pesachim 30a "Rabbi, the halachah: chametz," etc. — even though he does not hold like Rav that in its kind in any amount, but because it is something that has a permitted aspect; Rava according to his reason holds like R' Shimon as written there; and Rav needed to say on account of in its kind in any amount because he holds after Pesach like R' Yehudah as written there; and this is what Rambam wrote that the reason for chametz in any amount is because it is something that has a permitted aspect; but from R' Yochanan there it is difficult, for he holds like R' Shimon after its time, and nevertheless he holds in its time even in its kind in sixty — one must say R' Yochanan and Rava dispute in this (whether this is called something that has a permitted aspect, and we hold like Rava). But the first reasoning holds as Tosafot wrote there on account of the severity of chametz, etc. — see there s.v. "he said," etc.; and Orach Chayim siman 447 seif 2 stam like the first reasoning, and as Maggid Mishneh wrote (chapter 1 of Laws of Chametz law 5) that for Rambam even by taste in any amount — see there seif 9: (Excerpt) And there are those who disagree. As Rava said Pesachim 30a; and even though he holds all Torah prohibitions in sixty, as in Pesachim (57b) — from Rav it is not difficult that Rav needed to say according to his reasoning, because Rav holds like R' Yehudah after its time, and likewise Shmuel for clarity of the matter even like R' Yehudah; and this is what he said "not in its time," etc. like R' Shimon; but in its time, even for R' Yehudah and R' Yochanan one can say he holds like the rabbis who argue on R' Shimon — for that which is something that has a permitted aspect is R' Shimon's, and the rabbis argue on him (see in Sefer HaEshkol); and this is what is written in Avodah Zarah 73b "thus R' Yehudah," etc.; and Tosafot there s.v. "tevel," etc. — and one might ask, etc.; and therefore it comes according to the rabbis, and so they said there R' Yehudah, meaning except tevel, etc. [see Milchamot chapter Kamma] — and it appears specifically tevel; and if so, all that has a permitted aspect is likewise — and he says above "granted," etc. (end).`,
    '4#ל': `And not, etc. As above (s.k. 15) from chapter Orlah.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] Everything that has a permitted aspect, etc. — and even though from the Torah one against two is nullified, as it is written "follow the majority," the Sages were stringent regarding something that has a permitted aspect because there will be permission later — one may not eat it through nullification. Rashi Beitzah 3b. Taz s.k. 1. Peri Chadash letter 1. Levushei Srad letter 1. And see above siman 100 letter 1.`,
    '2#_': `(2) There. If it was mixed with others, whether whole, etc. — meaning that otherwise it would not be nullified, for it is a counted item and is not nullified, as stated in siman 86; and it teaches here that one should not err to think that a whole egg has only the stringency of a counted item — it teaches that there is also the stringency of something that has a permitted aspect. Taz s.k. 2. Levushei Srad letter 2. And there is a practical difference regarding doubt, as stated below. Taz there. And this is what is written in the next note.`,
    '3#_': `(3) There. And even if there is doubt whether it was laid on Yom Tov, etc. — in this matter something that has a permitted aspect is stricter than other things that are not nullified, for there we are lenient in their doubts if the prohibition is certainly d'rabbanan — we say in his doubt it is permitted like any d'rabbanan doubt, and it will not harm him if it is an important item; but here, even in a d'rabbanan prohibition doubt, it is forbidden since it has a permitted aspect. Taz s.k. 3. And so Peri Chadash letter 2 — something that has a permitted aspect, even in a d'rabbanan doubt, and even in a thousand is not nullified, since there is permission for its prohibition. Levushei Srad letter 4. Chokhmat Adam general rule 53 letter 19. And so is the opinion of the Shulchan Aruch in what it wrote: "and even if there is doubt whether it was laid on Yom Tov," which is a d'rabbanan doubt, and it was mixed with forbidden eggs. However, see in our words in Orach Chayim siman 513 letter 6, where we wrote that there is permission in a d'rabbanan doubt mixed with others, only that we wrote there to be stringent like the Shulchan Aruch and its reasoning, only for great need or great loss one may rely on the permission — see there.`,
    '4#_': `(4) There. And it was mixed with other forbidden items. And if from those mixtures it was mixed again with others — see below siman 110 seif 8 in the hagahah.`,
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#א': `Something that has a permitted aspect. See what Peleti wrote — why did it enter his mind to be lenient in a doubt of chadash on 16 Nisan — see there.`,
    '1#ב': `Even if there is doubt, etc. This is regarding an egg laid on Yom Tov after Shabbat, where there is a Torah prohibition — then a double doubt does not help, for there is doubt in the item itself and sixty in the mixture; but regarding an egg laid on ordinary Yom Tov, which is only a rabbinic decree of Yom Tov after Shabbat — we are not stringent in a double doubt; so is the opinion of Magen Avraham; and see what Peleti wrote in resolving the Gemara.`,
    '1#ג': `Not in its kind — regarding something that has a permitted aspect all agree specifically in its kind; and the reason — see what Peleti wrote in the name of Ran.`,
    '1#ד': `Nullified in sixty — and everyone follows the name, since it is forbidden in any amount without sixty.`,
    '1#ה': `If one whitened it — and Peri Chadash disagrees, that appearance in a rabbinic matter has no substance; and see what Peleti wrote — his words are not correct, for everyone agrees that since it came for appearance it is as its kind.`,
    '2#א': `There are those who say — they wrote in the language "there are those who say," even though we do not find explicitly in poskim who disagree; nevertheless, since Or Zarua gave the reason that therefore it is not a doubt called something that has a permitted aspect when Eliyahu comes, because the food spoils — we learn he does not hold this reasoning "who knows that Eliyahu will permit"; and what Shach and Peri Chadash wrote that so is implied from the Gemara — see what Peleti wrote — nothing is implied.`,
    '2#ב': `Therefore egg, etc. — and it is not a double doubt, for there is one doubt in the item and one in the mixture — it is not a double doubt.`,
    '2#ג': `Because the permitting matter — and what Tzemach Tzedek challenged in contradiction: if it will be permitted also now it is permitted, and if forbidden nevertheless permitted because nullified — see Peleti.`,
    '3#א': `Because one must, etc. — and Maharil disagrees, that in minor outlay it is called something that has a permitted aspect; and he gave a great principle: if the outlay is equal to the prohibition, even if the whole mixture is worth more, it is called great outlay; but in less it is called minor outlay and is within something that has a permitted aspect; and see Peleti — one should be concerned for Maharil.`,
    '3#ב': `To kasher it and to whiten it — it is possible the vessel cracked through whitening in fire and it is as food that spoils.`,
    '3#ג': `And all similar cases — and Shach's opinion is to wait until twelve months, then it returns to its permission from the Torah; and even so, d'rabbanan it is forbidden — see Peleti.`,
    '4#א': `Where the food spoils — and if it is not as esteemed as now we have no concern, since it is not spoiled; for an egg laid on Yom Tov is something that has a permitted aspect since one can wait until tomorrow — and the matter is known that a day-old egg is more esteemed.`,
    '4#ב': `But its taste is nullified — this is when there is no substance at all, such as placing an egg in the dish and removing it whole and only taste remains in the pot — this is permitted; but if the egg dissolved it is forbidden — see Peleti.`,
    '4#ג': `If its prohibition is not on account of itself — meaning: absorption itself cannot forbid, for it is kind in not its kind; only it is prohibition of benefit and it is in its kind. It teaches not like the opinion of R' Yehudah that the piece itself is called by the name of the absorbing prohibition that forbids it and is kind in not its kind.`,
    '4#ד': `Since it is permitted for roasting — and even though we say explicitly in chapter Orlah regarding terumah: even though one can give it to a kohen, nevertheless it is not something that has a permitted aspect, because the kohen was never forbidden; likewise here for roasting it was never forbidden — there for two people: for the kohen it was not forbidden and for the Israelite it was never permitted; unlike here for one person forbidden in cooking and permitted in roasting — one can well say it is something that has a permitted aspect, for we tell him "until you eat it in prohibition eat it in permission" — investigate.`,
    '4#ה': `That was not recognizable — such as a vat full of grapes in clusters and much wine already dripped from it before Shabbat, and on Shabbat too wine drips from them and it is little and nullified; only one might say it is something that has a permitted aspect — it teaches not, since the dripping wine on Shabbat was never recognizable — and see Peleti who says one can say otherwise first-first it is nullified.`,
    '4#ו': `For it is possible — and terumah is different, for there is no mitzvah to ask; but vows — there is a mitzvah to ask, for everyone who vows is as if he built a high place.`,
    '4#ז': `Becomes forbidden again — meaning that same thing, not that the prohibition returns like chadash where the body of the thing does not return, only the prohibition — and see Peleti at the beginning of this siman.`,
    '4#ח': `And there are those who disagree — even though we forbid chametz even mere taste, for in chametz they were stringent as in not its kind.`,
    '4#ט': `One who cooks on Shabbat — intentionally, forbidden to him forever and permitted to others — as written; since it is not permitted to him it is not called something that has a permitted aspect — see Peleti.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 102 in Shulchan Aruch seif 1 in the hagahah) However, if one whitened it in food or placed it in a pot, etc. Magen Avraham in Orach Chayim siman 513 s.k. 7 wrote: it appears that if it is not recognizable in appearance but only in taste it is nullified, even though it came to fix the pot, like water and salt in dough, etc.; and so is in Yoreh Deah siman 102 in Shulchan Aruch — specifically when one filled it with chicken, which is recognizable in appearance — then it is not nullified — end of his words; and it requires investigation, for as Shulchan Aruch states before us, on the contrary his words appear reversed; and it is possible another version of Shulchan Aruch was before him.`,
    '2#_': `(There seif 2) There are those who say that they only said something has a permitted aspect when the permitting matter will certainly come, etc. — therefore an egg of doubtful tereifah. Shach s.k. 4 wrote: I do not know why he wrote "there are those who say," etc. — and similarly is proved in the Gemara at the beginning of Beitzah which challenges, etc. — see there; and one who examines Rashi's words there will see this is not a challenge at all, for Rashi explained doubtful tereifah egg does not mean the doubt comes from the chicken having a tereifah doubt, but that there is an egg here and it is unknown whether from a tereifah or kosher one — and tereifah doubt as such is not something that has a permitted aspect [see Peri Megadim in Shaar HaTzedek s.k. 7 what he wrote explaining Shach's words]; and one can say according to what Rambam wrote that chametz on Pesach is something that has a permitted aspect — even though next year it will return to prohibition, nevertheless it is called something that has a permitted aspect since there is permission for its prohibition; and it is called something that has a permitted aspect — "until you eat it in prohibition eat it in permission." And behold we do not hold that a certainly tereifah egg is muktzeh because it is fit for a dog; nevertheless a doubtful tereifah egg appears to be muktzeh, since for him it is not fit because of prohibition, and for a dog too it is not fit, since what is fit for him he will not give to a dog — for if he keeps the chicken twenty-one days it is possible she will lay and become fit for him; therefore the Gemara's challenge is difficult; unless tereifah doubt is something that does not have a permitted aspect. The baraita that cites "and if mixed" would not deal only with doubt, but the baraita deals with all cases whether certain or doubtful; and even though it appears to R' Yehudah as a stretch that "if mixed" refers to certainty — namely certainty alone — but both certain and doubtful together this is not a stretch; if so there is no Gemara challenge — if it deals with doubtful tereifah, "if mixed" would also refer to certain tereifah; if so why is certain tereifah not nullified, for it is something that does not have a permitted aspect? Therefore the Gemara's challenge is from certainty; and one can say: behold Tzemach Tzedek challenges how can a tereifah egg be something that has a permitted aspect? Either way: if they return and lay, there is no prohibition at all; if they do not return and lay, there is no permitted aspect and it should nullify like other forbidden items. Granted regarding muktzeh it works well — even though tomorrow it will be permitted, nevertheless now it is called forbidden; unlike tereifah doubt — if it will be permitted also now there is no prohibition here at all; and it appears certain tereifah doubt in general is not called something that has a permitted aspect at all, like Tzemach Tzedek's challenge; and why Rashba and Tur needed to innovate that a doubtful tereifah egg is not something that has a permitted aspect because the permitting matter will not certainly come — one can say it deals with a doubtful tereifah egg mixed on Yom Tov or Shabbat, forbidden to move on account of muktzeh; and as stated, he holds something that will return to prohibition after time, even though it has permission in the interim, is not something that has a permitted aspect; if so, certainly on the first and second Shabbat within twenty-one days it is not something that has a permitted aspect, since for the coming Shabbat it will return to prohibition; but on the last Shabbat within twenty-one days one might say it is something that has a permitted aspect — for if they return and lay it will be permitted for the coming Shabbat — Rashba teaches: since the permitting matter will not certainly come — for perhaps they will not return and lay and the egg will remain in doubt until after twelve months and afterward too forbidden to move — therefore it is not something that has a permitted aspect at all; if so, according to this, Rashba's challenge is resolved — the baraita deals with doubtful tereifah in general; even if it deals with doubtful tereifah mixed on Shabbat or Yom Tov — for the baraita deals with every Shabbat — if so on the first and second Shabbat within twenty-one days why is it not nullified, for it is not something that has a permitted aspect at all? And the Gemara challenges well: behold it is something that does not have a permitted aspect — investigate well.`,
  },
  'mechaber': {
    '1#main': `The law of something that has a permitted aspect. And it has 4 seifim: Everything that has a permitted aspect, such as an egg laid on Yom Tov that is fit for tomorrow — if it was mixed with others, whether whole or beaten, it is not nullified even in a thousand; and even if there is a doubt whether it was laid on Yom Tov and it was mixed with others they are forbidden; and if it was mixed with what is not its kind it is nullified in sixty (Tur and Sh"D and responsa of Rosh and R' Yerucham and Hagahot Sh"D and Rashal and Rokeach and as stated Smag and Smak in laws of Yom Tov and likewise Or Zarua general rule 26 in the name of Smag and Taz general rule 43 and not like Bach): {Rama: However, if one whitened it in food or placed it in a pot to fix the pot, such as one filled it with chicken, it is not nullified (Hagahot Sh"D and Arukh); see Orach Chayim siman 513.}`,
    '2#main': `There are those who say that they only said something has a permitted aspect when the permitting matter will certainly come in any event, or if the permitting matter is in his hand to do without loss; but something that is not in his hand and it is not certain that the permitting matter will come — it is not in the law of something that has a permitted aspect; therefore an egg of doubtful tereifah that was mixed with others is not in the law of something that has a permitted aspect, because the permitting matter is not certain and is not in his hand.`,
    '3#main': `A vessel that became forbidden through absorption of a prohibition that was mixed with others and is not recognizable — it is nullified in the majority and we do not judge it like something that has a permitted aspect (because one must expend outlay to kasher it, and all similar cases).`,
    '4#main': `There are those who say that something that has a permitted aspect does not apply where the food spoils: {Rama: This that something that has a permitted aspect is not nullified applies only if the prohibition is in existence or there is still substance of the prohibition in the mixture; but its taste is nullified (Tur Orach Chayim siman 513 and Arukh and Hagahot Alfasi end of Avodah Zarah and Mahari in the name of Mahariach); and likewise if its prohibition is not on account of itself it is nullified — therefore a piece that was not salted within three days — even though some say it is called something that has a permitted aspect since it is permitted for roasting — nevertheless it is nullified, for it is forbidden only on account of blood absorbed in it (Taz siman 110); every prohibition that was not recognizable before it was mixed is nullified even though it is something that has a permitted aspect (Arukh general rule 25 in the name of seif 3 and Mordechai chapter 1 of Shabbat); one who vowed from one matter and it was mixed afterward — for him it is called something that has a permitted aspect (there and in Maimonides chapter 8 of Nedarim and in Ran chapter 1 and Or Zarua and this is Gemara Arukh chapter 7 of Nedarim daf 59 and Yerushalmi chapter 6 of Nedarim and consensus of poskim); for it is possible to ask about his vow. Something that has permission and becomes forbidden again, such as chametz on Pesach — is not called something that has a permitted aspect (Mordechai chapter Kamma); and there are those who disagree in this (Rambam chapter 15 of Forbidden Foods); and it is only called something that has a permitted aspect if it was permitted for the one who was forbidden, but if it remained forbidden for one forever even though permitted for others, such as one who cooks on Shabbat — it is not called something that has a permitted aspect (Beit Yosef in the name of R' Yerucham in the name of Ramah).}`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 102, Taz s.k. 10) "This is because his Mishnah overpowered him." All the Acharonim preceded him in this, as Shach wrote in s.k. 10; and what he wrote "but in the substance of the law he wrote well according to Orach Chayim," etc. — it is not so, as Siftei Kohen s.k. 9 wrote — for Orach Chayim also holds thus. And what he wrote "it is not called something that has a permitted aspect" — see Shach and Siftei Kohen at length there; and what Taz wrote that an unsalted piece is also something that has a permitted aspect is not as he wrote in s.k. 9 — end of his words.`,
  },
  'peleti': {
    '1#א': `And if it was mixed, etc. In Orach Chayim siman 513 seif 1-2 Shulchan Aruch wrote on an egg laid on Yom Tov: if mixed in a thousand all are forbidden; if doubt whether laid on Yom Tov or weekday — forbidden; Magen Avraham seif 5 wrote Shulchan Aruch reversed the order as the Tur wrote — there doubt whether laid, etc., and if mixed in a thousand that law also applies; but Mechaber switched to teach that regarding doubt, if mixed it is permitted as a double doubt — requires investigation; for in Yoreh Deah siman 102 Shulchan Aruch ruled to forbid even if the doubt was mixed; and possibly there it deals with Torah prohibition and Yom Tov after Shabbat, and in Orach Chayim with rabbinic prohibition and ordinary Yom Tov — thus one must understand: in Beitzah Gemara on that baraita "an egg laid on Yom Tov is forbidden," etc., "its doubt is forbidden; if mixed even in a thousand all are forbidden" — according to one who says egg laid on Yom Tov is forbidden rabbinically, its doubt is rabbinic and lenient; Gemara answers the doubt comes from doubtful tereifah — if so, why mention something that has a permitted aspect to nullify in majority? It answers: this tanna is of litra of pressed figs and holds everything customarily counted is not nullified; Rav Ashi said something that has a permitted aspect is not nullified even in rabbinic doubt — end. One should question Mechaber in contradiction: if Torah prohibition, double doubt forbidden because doubt in item and in mixture — if he deals with egg laid on Yom Tov per tanna of litra of figs, how teach "its doubt is forbidden; if mixed in a thousand forbidden" — in rabbinic we do not say double doubt? If we say as Rabbeinu Tam that "if mixed" refers to certainty not doubt — whence stringency in Torah doubt in item and mixture, for all R' Yehudah's stringencies are from that proof "if mixed in a thousand"? If "if mixed" refers to certainty per Rabbeinu Tam, there is no proof — difficult either way. One can say: if "its doubt is forbidden" deals with doubtful tereifah, why need "if mixed in a thousand" on certain tereifah — who mentions certain tereifah in the baraita teaching egg laid on Yom Tov forbidden, doubt meaning doubtful tereifah? Therefore above refers to doubtful tereifah forbidden if mixed in a thousand — but that is double doubt; one must say one doubt in item and one in mixture as R' Yehudah wrote; but for the answer that doubt means doubt of laid on Yom Tov — "if mixed in a thousand" refers to the beginning "egg laid on Yom Tov" which is certain, and that if mixed is forbidden; but in doubt it is not forbidden, for double doubt, and in rabbinic we do not forbid even one doubt in item and one in mixture. More reasonable: certainly per Rabbeinu Tam even one doubt in item and one in mixture is double doubt even for Torah, as Gemara regarding tereifah egg; R' Yehudah who forbids holds "if mixed" refers to doubt even in rabbinic — per his Gemara reasoning; only Shulchan Aruch was uncertain whether law is Rabbeinu Tam or R' Yehudah — therefore in Torah prohibition he was stringent like R' Yehudah forbidding double doubt; and so his words here; but in rabbinic prohibition he was lenient relying on Rabbeinu Tam who is lenient even for Torah — and so Orach Chayim; but Gemara passages explain whether "if mixed" refers to certainty for egg laid on Yom Tov or tereifah egg, or doubt applies to both — investigate. In Pilpul HaTalmidim I resolved Shulchan Aruch's words: he certainly holds like R' Yehudah that "if mixed" refers to doubt not certainty; but he was puzzled: since he did not know from "something that has a permitted aspect even in rabbinic not nullified" — if so, when challenging from "its doubt forbidden," challenge from "if mixed forbidden" — why not rabbinic doubt lenient? See Maggid Mishneh who wrote explicitly they depend on each other. Further: why was he puzzled why egg laid on Yom Tov forbidden — he holds doubtful prepared forbidden? Answer that muktzeh is like Torah is strained; doubtful fallen fruit is also doubtful prepared, same decree for egg — why didn't R' Nissim challenge? Reason muktzeh like Torah only per R' Nissim — otherwise challenge removed from Mishnah distinguishing chicken; therefore he knew something that has a permitted aspect: even in rabbinic we say doubt stringent, only we do not say two doubts; if this doubt mixed with others and law would nullify, it is not nullified because something that has a permitted aspect twice we do not say "until you eat in prohibition" on beginning of its doubt and again on its mixture — we do not say this; if so no challenge from doubtful prepared — he knew something that has a permitted aspect; likewise from "if mixed" one need not say something that has a permitted aspect; but difficult: it teaches "its doubt forbidden" — why, rabbinic doubt lenient? Cannot say because something that has a permitted aspect — if so why "if mixed forbidden" — we do not say two doubts; unlike if egg forbidden for preparation of Rav — then doubt is by law not because something that has a permitted aspect — one can say even in a thousand nullified because something that has a permitted aspect once we say; Gemara answers we come to doubtful tereifah, etc., concludes tanna of litra of figs holds egg is counted item not nullified; Rav Ashi: now that we came here, one can say Yom Tov doubt weekday doubt, something that has a permitted aspect, even rabbinic doubt forbidden; no challenge: if so nullify in a thousand — we do not say two doubts? No — not nullified is not from something that has a permitted aspect but from counted item — something that has a permitted aspect properly forbids its doubt. Possible this is Sefer HaTerumah's reason ruling like tanna of litra of figs — per Rav Ashi's ending: Orach Chayim permitted not because double doubt — he ruled Yoreh Deah siman 110 no double doubt — only they did not forbid from something that has a permitted aspect. Now Shulchan Aruch's words are correct in Yoreh Deah: Torah doubt something that has a permitted aspect like chadash — Magen Avraham ruled its doubt and mixed in a thousand forbidden; to forbid its doubt no need something that has a permitted aspect — Torah doubt stringent — thus something that has a permitted aspect helps forbid mixture even in a thousand. Unlike egg rabbinic; for us egg is not counted item — if you forbid its doubt because something that has a permitted aspect, cannot forbid mixture from two doubts. Bach and Peri Chadash brought Mordechai's challenge: how did Gemara Menachot say for one who holds chadash rabbinic, eating on 17 Nisan is rabbinic doubt — behold something that has a permitted aspect? Ri answered chadash returns to prohibition next year, not something that has a permitted aspect; Peri Chadash challenged: Yerushalmi and many places chadash is something that has a permitted aspect; grain permitted by omer never returns to this prohibition — unlike chametz on Pesach when Pesach returns. Well said until puzzling how hidden from Ri and Mordechai — egg laid on Yom Tov should be something that has a permitted aspect next Yom Tov when another egg laid. Therefore Ri seems to hold like Yerushalmi Tosafot Rosh brought: from entering Israel all grain even in ancient cisterns became chadash; for us chadash rabbinic, mitzvah ceased when Temple destroyed, chadash returns — all grain forbidden, day of waving in exile does not help like at entry; chadash we hold rabbinic — therefore reason all day forbidden until Temple rebuilt — returns to prohibition, not certainly something that has a permitted aspect; see Orach Chayim: some hold doubt is something that has a permitted aspect perhaps Eliyahu comes — they wrote spoiled food, all the more say so to be lenient — that was his intent. But truly this answer is strained; if so we have no remnant except Mordechai's answer we are expert in festival fixing. But difficult: if so egg laid on second day Yom Tov should be permitted — we are expert in month fixing permitted like chadash for rabbinic; and certainly for rabbinic must say Menachot conclusion we do not eat on 16th because something that has a permitted aspect — implies conclusion we do not say expert fixing; but for Torah chadash no proof — difficult; say since we hold preparation Torah, all born on Yom Tov even not Yom Tov after Shabbat is Torah category, unlike Maharsha who holds "granted for preparation" deals only Yom Tov after Shabbat — all included in Sages' fixing Torah prohibition, only so Gemara for Torah chadash; more: things forbidden on account of Yom Tov — Sages fixed second Yom Tov lest one make Yom Tov weekday and belittle festivals like Torah — otherwise now too make weekday; but chadash 17 Nisan does not depend on Yom Tov, only month fixing — we say we are expert, Sages did not decree — investigate well.`,
    '1#ב': `Not in its kind nullified in sixty. Rambam wrote it appears to me, etc.; Raavad wondered — behold complete Mishnah in Nedarim: not in its kind in sixty; vows are something that has a permitted aspect. It appears the distinction between in its kind and not: in its kind the prohibition aspect is not nullified because taste remains as before; therefore in not its kind prohibition taste passed and no trace — therefore even something that has a permitted aspect is nullified, for its aspect already nullified. This is wet; but dry-with-dry each stands alone — what difference in kind or not, not nullified, each stands alone; in not its kind easier to recognize prohibition — thus room to say and be stringent; therefore Nedarim deals explicitly with wet in sixty — thus well to distinguish; but dry-with-dry no proof — he brought tevel as stam matter even dry-with-dry.`,
    '1#ג': `If one whitened the food. Peri Chadash elaborated: this appearance should not have been resolved; in rabbinic they concluded leniently as we say in blood of defeat. I did not understand his challenge on something that has a permitted aspect — they forbade all rabbinic like Torah and its doubt stringent until Gemara challenges on adding wood, etc.; R' Eliezer who says rabbinic something that has a permitted aspect — per Rosh's explanation the challenge: should not prohibition of something that has a permitted aspect nullify like Torah — no need inside. But Pri Toar wrote appearance helps only in benefit prohibitions — well said; but none of their words apply here — certainly appearance has no substance; nevertheless a little exists; in something that has a permitted aspect a little forbids — at least a little mixed; only deals not in its kind — therefore nullified, not recognizable, taste nullified; but appearance is recognizable and returns to in its kind; also for taste it is in its kind, taste also recognizable — appearance is like for taste, customary to put in dish, like for taste — investigate.`,
    '2#_': `Only when the permitting matter, etc. Brought in responsa Tzemach Tzedek: contradiction — if forbidden it nullifies; if permitted, retroactively permitted — this reasoning explained Orach Chayim general rule 25 rule 18: Avodah Zarah prohibition forbidden through Torah doubt nullified in sixty though possibly permitted when Eliyahu comes — not called something that has a permitted aspect because then retroactively never forbidden — unlike egg and tevel forbidden with permission — end; and Rashba wrote not called something that has a permitted aspect unless permitting matter certain — end; thus he mentioned this reasoning. Tzemach Tzedek: nevertheless l'chatchila wait without relying on nullification — his words unclear if later known certainly forbidden, permitted to eat because nullified in its measure while now doubtful until "until you eat in prohibition" — no understanding. In sharp reasoning: per egg in shell not fit for dog — doubtful tereifah egg on Shabbat forbidden to move, not fit for man or dog; if mixed on Shabbat, though tereifah nullifies in majority, muktzeh on Shabbat not nullified — can wait until tomorrow. But this is not so — returns to prohibition next Shabbat, muktzeh again, not something that has a permitted aspect; therefore doubtful prohibition also muktzeh as Maharsha on calf eight days on Shabbat — we do not say retroactive permission; doubtful tereifah mixed Shabbat when that week chicken completes 21 days — not nullified from muktzeh; if returns prohibition next Shabbat not certain — perhaps clarified kosher, now still muktzeh as Maharsha and as they said firstborn fell in pit — though blemish revealed, muktzeh; next Shabbat permitted, fit, no muktzeh — if so forbidden; Rashba: not clear permitting matter comes — not something that has a permitted aspect — like prohibition returning next Shabbat nullifies — clear though not Rashba's language — they said to us thus he wrote simply. Also: perhaps per Rabbenu Shimshon who holds Rabbeinu Tam one doubt item one mixture permitted from double doubt — nevertheless something that has a permitted aspect we do not say so — see below siman 110. Treifah milk cheese mixed — doubt gored or fallen, checkable after slaughter — permitted, Rabbeinu Tam double doubt; not something that has a permitted aspect — if slaughtered, clarified possibly permitted — something that has a permitted aspect does not help double doubt — do not rely on nullification permission; not difficult: if known forbidden, also nullified in majority — either way eat? No — if clarified tereifah not eaten; all permission is double doubt; if clarified tereifah only mixture doubt — treated as something that has a permitted aspect — teaches we do not say so since permitting matter not certain. Rashba strained. After investigation: Ran wrote therefore something that has a permitted aspect in its kind not nullified — permission in permission in its kind nullification inapplicable — same kind; only prohibition in permission, prohibition causes as not its kind; something that has a permitted aspect like permission — returns in its kind not nullified; if clarified possible permission also called something that has a permitted aspect — doubt also permission name, prohibition not decided, hope to permit, doubt permitted — called permission name, returns in its kind not nullified; if prohibition name to this also permission name — before called clear prohibition, nevertheless name people call permission waiting to be permitted, not nullified per this reasoning; if clarified forbidden, not nullified retroactively — we say even if later known certainly forbidden, not nullified retroactively — then had permission name, in its kind not nullified; afterward prohibition name — clear. Rashba teaches: since no clear permission, called prohibition name, nullifies. Vainly all wise hearts stumbled on plain words. Shach challenged Rashba: obvious — otherwise why Gemara challenge in Beitzah: granted Yom Tov weekday doubt, something that has a permitted aspect, if mixed even thousand forbidden; unlike tereifah doubt — why if mixed all forbidden? Difficult — also something that has a permitted aspect. Rashi explained tereifah doubt doubt born from tereifah chicken — not doubt if chicken tereifah, doubt in egg which chicken; Rashi wrote because dealing egg laid Yom Tov teaches tereifah doubt forbidden — if doubt in chicken, no egg matter — chicken forbidden egg permitted impossible. Must be doubt in egg alone — two chickens one tereifah one kosher, egg from one of them, unknown — all doubt in egg, comparable to beginning egg; Shach's challenge impossible to clarify forever. Also: Rashba's law permitting matter not certain is per R' Yehudah double doubt — for R' Yehudah "if mixed" doubt; per Rashi "if mixed" general, egg its doubt forbidden, mixed that doubt or certainty thousand forbidden — not only doubt because something that has a permitted aspect. For one who holds even returning prohibition is something that has a permitted aspect like Rambam chametz — why challenge on tereifah doubt forbidden if mixed — whole baraita Yom Tov — tereifah forbidden on Yom Tov, not only eating obvious, even moving not fit as Maharsha — forbidden moving as baraita teaches forbidden to move — even mixed thousand something that has a permitted aspect — investigate baraita language, no challenge what tereifah here, why teach tereifah forbidden obvious Torah doubt stringent — Gemara assumes certain tereifah egg fit for dogs, only doubt not as Maharsha — therefore need say "if mixed" only doubt not certainty — Gemara does not hold; Shach resolved. Also: Gemara rabbinic doubt lenient even something that has a permitted aspect — implies Sages did not fix like Torah — obvious Rashba's law only rabbinically not nullified Torah majority — when doubt if something that has a permitted aspect, all rabbinic doubt lenient except Rashba's doubt stringent after we learned R' Eliezer something that has a permitted aspect even rabbinic doubt stringent — Sages fixed nail like Torah — therefore if doubt will have permission, also not nullified since Sages fixed nail like Torah — teaches "it appears" no Gemara proof, even rabbinic doubt lenient — investigate. In novellae: per Tosafot Kirah R' Yochanan egg forbidden fluids that flowed — egg permitted moving — see Rosh end Beitzah; therefore baraita egg Shabbat Yom Tov forbidden moving — Torah told us no muktzeh; R' Yochanan has muktzeh — not fit eating, muktzeh name, forbidden moving — baraita is R' Yochanan; no Shach challenge tereifah something that has a permitted aspect — R' Yochanan holds tereifah lays eggs — see Chullin, idolaters tereifah lays, all the more live tereifah; something that has a permitted aspect even if wait lay within 21 days still in prohibition, permitting matter never comes — Gemara challenges mixed thousand why forbidden; for us tereifah does not lay — Rashba uncertain until ram — investigate.`,
    '3#_': `Vessel that became forbidden, etc. Rashal gave reason not something that has a permitted aspect since not automatic; so Assembly of Elders Bava Metzia 54 in Ran; Shach s.k. 8 wondered — vows etc. not time-dependent nevertheless something that has a permitted aspect — resolved: in vows Nedarim 59a explicitly because mitzvah to ask — therefore reason where not time-dependent only action, perhaps no action, no permission — with mitzvah presumption Israel zealous in mitzvot — maaser sheni mitzvah bring or redeem Rambam bless on it; hekdesh tevel must separate redeem — Ran kodshim; but no mitzvah from Torah to be something that has a permitted aspect — no challenge on Rashal. Rashba: need outlay — derived chapter HaZahav 53 maaser sheni nullified in majority — which maaser? Without worth perutah entered Jerusalem exited — on entered exited not something that has a permitted aspect — bring in; if without perutah Gemara would not challenge — implies above Yom Tov outlay not something that has a permitted aspect; here entered exited implies even one step — Tosafot Assembly many authors; per Rashal above resolve maaser without perutah no mitzvah bring redeem — time-dependent not something that has a permitted aspect; see Maharsha not like Rashi above tamei — not in baraita; understand Gemara doubled partitions — second challenge absorb not enough; without perutah no challenge can separate at all; entered Jerusalem well challenges fell partitions rabbinic absorb — Rashba difficult. Resolve: without perutah deals fell partitions — otherwise repair through bringing — Gemara challenge bring prohibition redeem half perutah today no Temple courtyard redeem worth mana perutah Rambam Shulchan Aruch — investigate. Maharil responsum 134 disagrees hagalah minor — derives Tosafot distinguish great small outlay — Tosafot s.v. return bring in above without perutah no challenge deals far from Jerusalem no permission; here entered exited implies even one step — difference great small outlay, one step called has permission. Challenge Maharil: he explains even one step outlay since body maaser not perutah — why Tosafot far Jerusalem, applies less than perutah value small outlay also hinders. Appears above redeem on first money and answer not separate challenge bring maaser not separate redeem standing to separate — must say deals never had second tithe only less than perutah — still certainly maaser in hand — challenge bring without perutah together Jerusalem — small outlay not help, body maaser great — Tosafot far Jerusalem if so even all second tithe we do not say. Per this Tosafot conclusion even one step still challenges Maharil — deals maaser with perutah — why small outlay; Tosafot wanted end establish maaser without perutah worth entered exited — challenge return bring — small outlay, cannot say another matter already redeemed presume — entered Jerusalem still question; therefore stam even one step no outlay — Tosafot clear like Maharil; reconciled not contradict Tosafot Nedarim 108 maaser called something that has a permitted aspect can bring redeem — stam far until outlay; per Maharil correct, Rosh on me. Also Rashba another way without great/small outlay: redeem for him — if through another's trickery why Gemara challenge redeem on first money — he did not redeem, other went — stam he redeems, adds fifth mitzvah, cannot lose only bring or add fifth redeem — standing l'chatchila — certainly maaser second tithe plain way no outlay bring more than fifth sold this muktzeh; if so saying outlay bring loses wages unlike maaser without perutah not obligated give fifth — any outlay fraud — not something that has a permitted aspect — Gemara does not challenge bring has outlay; entered Jerusalem deals even with perutah — well challenges return bring outlay not more than fifth — investigate. Possibly Tosafot not hidden only conclusion two without perutah entered exited — challenges return bring has outlay fifth none — forced even one step no outlay. Anyway even Maharil calculate all per prohibition cooked inside — absorption not by vessel quality but absorption inside no perutah — known outlay exceeds value — Maharil vain small relative absorption; perhaps Maharil vessel becomes nevelah need outlay whole vessel — Rashba does not hold — hard split ancients. Rambam maaser sheni mixed hullin eat all purity place or redeem — if mixed Jerusalem forbids any amount in its kind, in Jerusalem like something that has a permitted aspect eat all purity — Peri Chadash inferred reason in Jerusalem outside no permission burdensome redeem bring — end maaser entered exited fell partitions implies maaser even must bring is something that has a permitted aspect — Peri Chadash split great small outlay — did not examine: in land second tithe to Israel if mixed obvious something that has a permitted aspect that prohibition for layman permitted redemption bring; mixed Jerusalem eat purity as maaser — never forbidden except eat not pure — no eternal permission though bring — not something that has a permitted aspect, Jerusalem sanctity stringent like something that has a permitted aspect must eat pure — Yerushalmi made Jerusalem like something that has a permitted aspect — truly no permission rule, only Jerusalem purity — Rambam Bikkurim 2 end Sages gave mixtures two things Yerushalmi alone like something that has a permitted aspect can eat there holiness — "made Jerusalem like something that has a permitted aspect" lest say something that has a permitted aspect forbids more — Jerusalem forbids more — strain clear no real permission rule, made Jerusalem like something that has a permitted aspect — Rambam not only Jerusalem, real permission everywhere — clear. No clear proof Rambam inclination — balanced — l'chatchila stringent hagalah minor outlay. Per Rabbenu Shimshon no double doubt something that has a permitted aspect — why rule vessel not ben yomo from double doubt — vessel something that has a permitted aspect double doubt not help — especially vessel not something that has a permitted aspect Peri Chadash — recognize prohibition through dish without hagalah how recognize — certainly absorption into dish, hagalah if cooked prohibition permission taste absorbed no prohibition taste — permitted rely this cook again — we say chapter 1 emitted, chapter 1 not emitted — no recognition; hagalah you increase outlay — strain intent only taste kapelia not hagalah — not applicable if cook imparts taste Bach. Shach: since tomorrow Torah permitted, something that has a permitted aspect Torah though rabbinic forbidden — like chametz Pesach — correct lenient within day though after Pesach rabbinic forbidden. Since put sharp thing forbids — not something that has a permitted aspect need fully permitted; nevertheless correct with Shach Torah lenient within day, afterward muktzeh rely Rashba; sharp cook concern not so concerned — sharp not Torah law.`,
    '4#א': `But its taste nullified. Shach extensively answered — though Mahariach in Hagahot Rosh Avodah Zarah — contradicts Gemara Nedarim; I did not know — Ran's challenge why forbidden wine cooked nosen taam contradicts his words; Gemara no contradiction — per his words no need split in kind not in kind; Rambam resolved distinguishing in kind not in kind; Raavad why not from Nedarim Mishnah — per Mahariach there only taste not something that has a permitted aspect; from chapter HaZahav maaser nullified majority which maaser — tamei uncommon, not plain dissolved — Tosafot Chullin 97; maaser nullified majority — perhaps Mahariach like Rashi Chullin even not in kind nosen taam terumah need 101; per Rashi even in kind need 101 terumah — establish maaser not in kind; praise maaser nullified majority baraita; dissolved need 60 wet wet even in kind. From Yevamot difficult — establish sin-offering piece dissolved, end pure sin-offering not offered — R' Ashi: taste something that has a permitted aspect, dissolved prohibition not in existence — "R' Eliezer fiction" — if so even dissolved — strained or dissolved means split thin pieces not piece worthy of honor but exist — not Mahariach dissolution; or Mahariach prohibition whole beforehand dissolved in dish new faces nullified if beforehand crushed returned water — prohibition unchanged no new faces — resolves Mahariach; practically Shach correct poskim. Shach wants distinction: prohibition body remains pot only dissolved — poskim forbid, proofs above; if removed body only taste — room permit — Hagahot Rosh Chullin rabbis 60 Rabbeinu Tam substance not in existence only taste nullified though permission — Mahariach Mordechai every hour Rabbeinu Tam not in existence only taste not something that has a permitted aspect. Room lenient no poskim against except why Rashba vessel permit — only taste; Shach wrote; Assembly Elders Bava Metzia brought vessel absorption Rabbeinu Tam — Rabbeinu Tam contradicts — say judging absorption absorption anyway never permitted — what emits something that has a permitted aspect — all on vessel body mixed vessels judging prohibition body. Understand Tur Yoreh Deah egg Yom Tov in dish Rabbeinu Tam in kind only not in kind taste 60 — nullified 60; Orach Chayim 513 same case — per Rabbeinu Tam something that has a permitted aspect not nullified only in existence taste 60 nullified permitted 60 — contradict; Shach wanted same intent both — strange different matters; reconcile: why not remove egg bodies from dish — certainly egg in dish crushed gave taste dish two prohibitions from egg — crushed body prohibition eating body; egg gives taste turns permitted forbidden — even remove egg body; Tur permits dish all sides — two reasons: forbid mixture egg bodies — not in kind; appearance taste like in kind — we judge crushed egg body not taste — could remove crushed egg taste remains — body not in kind nullified; forbid egg taste — in kind for taste like water salt — Tur: taste alone Sages did not decree something that has a permitted aspect — dish permitted all sides both one intent. If egg with permission in dish — egg absorbed dish taste — nosen taam; Rabbeinu Tam everywhere nosen taam; absorbed in egg no 60 against egg as mother's milk nevelah milk — not in kind forbidden; returns emits in kind not nullified — forbidden from egg like egg called not in kind — R' Yehudah; Rabbeinu Tam disagrees Tosafot drop milk Rashbam Ran Rabbeinu Tam brother Ran agreed. Say no mixed egg mass only dissolved taste cannot absorb return emit — only lump quantity; since absorbed in egg not receiving absorbs egg substance something that has a permitted aspect — absorbed egg taste taste not something that has a permitted aspect no nosen taam. Intent only in existence then not in kind helps; taste nullified split in kind not in kind — Tur Yoreh Deah did not mention — Yoreh Deah ruled no chanan other prohibitions — only not in kind; Orach Chayim not concluded no chanan — wrote mere taste — practical removed prohibition only taste lenient Rabbeinu Tam per R' Chaim Kohen especially rabbinic. Rabbeinu Tam proof Yevamot R' Ashi sin-offering piece 100 hullin — not offered something that has a permitted aspect; challenge Mishnah Orlah 100 101 cooked desire food permitted even zarim 101 no taste Rosh — forbidden priests R' Eliezer — remove meat only taste not something that has a permitted aspect — proven.`,
    '4#ב': `And if prohibition not on account of itself, etc. Shach extensively challenged — in hagahah admitted obvious Rama case not something that has a permitted aspect — called blood name R' Yehudah — not in kind. Even Rabbeinu Tam Ran piece in kind — this case no chanan concern — piece not cooked can eat raw — either unseparated blood in meat — no chanan; after cooking chanan — no longer something that has a permitted aspect no remedy — need say absorption forbids — Rama raised such taste not forbid something that has a permitted aspect — especially prohibition absorbed no remedy except emit from permitted — Rama well wrote; from his words learn if cooked egg Yom Tov in egg then fell in dish — does not forbid — challenges us chanan — that egg forbidden forbids next egg something that has a permitted aspect in substance — true not in Rama words for permission.`,
    '4#ג': `That was not recognizable, etc. This law requires investigation — Mordechai Semak wrote, Shulchan Aruch Orach Chayim 320 — per Mordechai end Chullin R' Meir on cow drooled blood — impossible without saliva drops — not nullified majority because not recognizable no nullification — see shechita 14 — seems opposite — dismiss: there forbidden nullifier not recognizable mouth one opening; zav semen impossible without zav drops together — unlike here wine stood before Shabbat Shabbat grape drip nullified immediately permitted recognizable not prohibition. From coin in pouch hekdesh — Tosafot challenged should nullify majority — wrote even something that has a permitted aspect only rabbinic — Kessef Mishneh truly something that has a permitted aspect — difficult prohibition never recognizable, mouth hekdesh immediately nullified pouch, permitted before — nevertheless something that has a permitted aspect. With all, vat wine drip Shabbat grape wine permit like yeast fell pit first-first nullified until 60 revives — grape drip first-first nullified no smaller — see 334; Ran all prohibitions so; Tosafot there; law not something that has a permitted aspect needs investigation — Mordechai analogy palm branches burned vessels benefit — therefore disputed.`,
    '4#ד': `Becomes forbidden again, etc. Rama in Torat Chatat ruled this opinion; Rashal wrote what great one to rule against Rambam. I think Rambam also concedes — difficult R' Yochanan chametz in sixty, why R' Yochanan something that has a permitted aspect not nullified — chametz permission, R' Yochanan explicitly after Pesach permitted R' Shimon, stam in sixty even seventh Pesach not spoiled wait evening — strange R' Yochanan Rava dispute whether returning prohibition is something that has a permitted aspect. Say Rambam: generally something that has a permitted aspect only in kind chametz severity; if prohibition returns in chametz severity karet — like not in kind; Ran: something that has a permitted aspect not nullified permission on it, permission in kind not nullified — holds stringencies since prohibition returned fixed prohibition name — prohibition and permission two kinds — chametz forbidden even not in kind also returning prohibition; R' Yochanan not stringent chametz like other prohibitions — not returning prohibition — therefore sixty; Rava stringent even not in kind in kind something that has a permitted aspect not nullified — Rambam Gemara. Therefore Shmuel also in kind any amount — R' Yochanan; if so dry dry R' Yehudah permitted — difficult something that has a permitted aspect — how Shmuel argue this law — per explanation fine since Shmuel did not decree not in kind like in kind, this too not — no real decisive proof.`,
    '4#ה': `But if remains, etc. Language confused: intentional cooker Shabbat forbidden him forever, others after Shabbat permitted; if mixed for others on Shabbat all forbid — has permission for him; if forbid even nullifier intentional — no permission Orach Chayim beginning 318 — Rosh wrote bikkurim mixed though kohen remedy Jerusalem, nazir no remedy — zarim not something that has a permitted aspect; scratch from Rambam commentary Mishnah chapter 4 Bikkurim forbids even zarim as kohen something that has a permitted aspect. Already wrote above — only Jerusalem made Jerusalem like something that has a permitted aspect severity Jerusalem — as elaborated; Rambam explicit chapter 4 Bikkurim hullin mixed Jerusalem forbids any amount — clear; Jerusalem stringent only — Rosh that person not something that has a permitted aspect — no doubt.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `It was mixed with others. See in responsa at the end of his Shabbat siddur responsum 3, where he was uncertain about something that has a permitted aspect that was mixed, and afterward one of them explained whether we say "everything that separated," etc. — that it is not relevant to say "until you eat it in prohibition eat it after Yom Tov in permission," for we presume there is no prohibition at all; or we do not say at all regarding something that has a permitted aspect that we follow the majority even in such a case — and he elaborated on this; and in the end he concluded that even in such a case we do not say "everything that separated," etc.; and this is also within the rule of something that has a permitted aspect; and so is implied in responsa of Tzemach Tzedek siman 69 in the middle of the responsum — see there; and so wrote Magen Avraham in siman 513 s.k. 13; and see in Tzelach on Beitzah 4 Yom Tov 2b s.v. "and since he elaborated to bring proof that we say regarding something that has a permitted aspect 'everything that separated returns to the majority'" — see there; and so appears to be the opinion of Chavot Yair in siman 233 — see there.`,
    '2#_': `It was mixed not in its kind. See in responsa of Radbaz part 2 siman 217.`,
    '3#_': `There are those who say. See Shach; and see in responsa Zichron Yosef Yoreh Deah siman 26; and see what my uncle the great rabbi R' Elazar, may his memory be blessed, wrote in Chemdat Yamim discourse 6; and in responsa Keter Kehunah siman 45; and in responsa Toldot Yitzchak siman 23; and see in the novellae of the Gaon R' Yonatan, may his memory be blessed, on Laws of Yom Tov of Rambam chapter 2 law 1; and in Bnei Chayya what he wrote on this; and see further in Shaar HaMelech chapter 8 of Laws of Forbidden Foods rule 11 what he wrote on this.`,
    '4#_': `Will certainly come in any event. See in responsa Tzemach Tzedek siman 69 on a doubtful tereifah egg laid on Yom Tov that was mixed — the questioner ruled it is not something that has a permitted aspect, since even after Yom Tov there will be no permission without nullification on account of tereifah; and he, may his memory be blessed, disagrees, for nevertheless it will certainly come to permission somewhat, for after Yom Tov the inclusive prohibition of being born will be removed and everything possible to do in permission we do — see there; and so agreed Peri Chadash and Menachem Tziyon general rule 74 sign 12; and see Peri Megadim who wrote similarly regarding one who said "konam on me eating this nevelah" and it was mixed — vow prohibition is in any case something that has a permitted aspect and is not nullified even in sixty; one can say vow prohibition does not apply at all since it is not in a mixture — nevertheless there are two prohibitions, only that there is not one prohibition from two sources, meaning to receive two sets of lashes or two sins; or it is called something that has a permitted aspect — see there; and see in responsa of Rabbeinu Akiva Eiger siman 65 who was asked on this law and initially also wanted to say that nevertheless vow prohibition is something that has a permitted aspect; however not for Peri Megadim's reason but since there is a dispute of the rabbis whether a vow takes effect on something already forbidden (see below siman 276 seif 5) — its doubt is stringent — if so it is something that has a permitted aspect; and he again questioned this, for nevertheless there is room to permit in our case, since that vows are something that has a permitted aspect is explained in the Gemara because there is a mitzvah to ask — if so in our case it is obvious there is no mitzvah, etc. — see the full responsum.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 102 seif 2) Or if the permitting matter is in his hand to do it. See in Kessef Mishneh (chapter 7 law 10 of Laws of Idolatry).`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 4) Where the food spoils. Rashal in Yesh Seder (Chullin chapter 8 siman 87) disagrees — that this too is called something that has a permitted aspect.`,
    '3#_': `(There in the hagahah) Every prohibition. It appears the opinion of Tosafot in Bava Kamma (daf 69) s.v. "everything gathered" is not so; and Magen Avraham already hinted to this (siman 318 s.k. 8); also so appears from Ran from what he wrote in Nedarim (daf 58a) on that which R' Shimon said "I too only said for removal" — see there: from the law of something that has a permitted aspect, even growths that were not recognizable by themselves are not nullified in the essence — see there.`,
    '4#_': `(There) That was not recognizable. From this one can infer that likewise if wheat flour mixed with barley flour and afterward he vowed from wheat flour — it is nullified; although vows are something that has a permitted aspect, nevertheless at the beginning of the prohibition's formation it is mixed and the name of prohibition was never called upon it — so it appears simple, not as written in Mekor Chayim — see there at length.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["Such as an egg, etc. See above siman 86 s.k. 9 and siman 110 s.k. 62."]`,
    '1#ב': `["And even if there is doubt whether it was laid on Yom Tov, etc. All that the Bach wrote here is not necessary at all — see there; also what he relied on regarding something that has a permitted aspect in a doubtful tereifah egg that was mixed is not correct, for it should be said this case is different since there is permission for its prohibition — and his words would make Semag and Semak contradict one another; rather as explained, and so is the opinion of the Rav in Taz general rule 43 to distinguish in this; and so I found explicitly in Mordechai chapter Kol HaTzalmim; also what he brought from Ran is nothing, for he only wrote there that a doubt regarding prepared on the second day of Yom Tov is permitted — and the Mechaber himself wrote so in Orach Chayim siman 497 seif 4; not so here, as below siman 110 s.k. 55; also what he wrote that in two mixtures one may permit — it should be said for all it is not so, as written in siman 110 seif 6 — see there."]`,
    '1#ג': `["And if it was mixed not in its kind, etc. It appears that here we follow the name and not the taste — if it is equal in name, even though not equal in taste, it is its kind and is not nullified; and if it is not equal in name, even though equal in taste, it is not its kind and is nullified — for we hold like Rava at the end of Avodah Zarah (daf 66) that we follow the name and not the taste; and so is implied explicitly from what Tosafot Pesachim (daf 97a) and the Rosh end of Avodah Zarah and other poskim wrote — that for us, who hold all prohibitions whether in its kind or not require sixty, what Rava said that in its kind we follow the name means regarding yayin nesech and tevel and tevel that is not nullified because it is something that has a permitted aspect, as in chapter 7 of Nedarim (beginning daf 58) and in Rambam chapter 15 of Forbidden Foods and Rashba in Torat HaBayit and in all other poskim; and so is implied in Ran end of Avodah Zarah that regarding something that has a permitted aspect it is not nullified in its kind — we follow the name — see there and investigate; and so Maharshal wrote explicitly in Orach Chayim siman 84 and Yoreh Deah siman 88."]`,
    '1#ד': `["Not in its kind it is nullified in sixty. Even though regarding beriah and a piece worthy of honor there is no distinction whether mixed in its kind or not, as above siman 101 seif 6 in the hagahah — this case is different, for the importance is not on account of the prohibition itself but because there will later be permission — the Sages did not want it nullified; and everything mixed not in its kind — the permission is not called on the forbidden item but on the name of what it was mixed into, and it is as if it has no permission. So too Torat Chatat general rule 40 rule 6."]`,
    '1#ה': `["However, if one whitened it, etc. Since it is for appearance it is not nullified, as in Orach Chayim siman 513 seif 3 — see there."]`,
    '1#ו': `["Or one placed it in a pot to fix, etc. The reason Maharai wrote there: everything placed one in another to fix is called its kind, like water and salt in dough — considered its kind since they fix the dough — as Tosafot chapter 2 of Beitzah (beginning daf 39) wrote."]`,
    '2#_': `There are those who say, etc. I do not know why the Mechaber wrote this law as "there are those who say," for Rashba and the Tur hold thus and none disagree; and so is implied in the Gemara at the beginning of Beitzah, which challenges: if one says an egg laid on Yom Tov is forbidden d'rabbanan — from where? For a baraita teaches and its doubt is forbidden; and if mixed in a thousand all are forbidden — why is its doubt forbidden, for it is a d'rabbanan doubt? And it answers that it deals with a doubtful tereifah egg; and it challenges: if so, say the latter clause "if mixed in a thousand all are forbidden" — if not, let it be a doubt of Yom Tov and a doubt of weekday, which is something that has a permitted aspect, for even in a thousand it is not nullified; if not, let it be a doubtful tereifah, which is something that does not have a permitted aspect and is nullified in its majority, etc. — now what is the challenge? Perhaps that clause dealing with mixing in a thousand refers to a doubtful tereifah egg? Rather certainly in all such cases it is not something that has a permitted aspect — investigate.`,
    '3#_': `Vessel, etc. "Because one must," etc. Maharshal wrote in Yoreh Deah siman 88 rule 6 that he did not need this reason of spending money, for even without that, since permission does not come automatically through time it is not considered something that has a permitted aspect — end of his words; and I did not grasp his intent, for even if permission comes without time it is considered something that has a permitted aspect, such as vows and the like; and Raah in Beit HaBechirah daf 128b challenges Rashba and says here there is another reason — it has no nullification except on account of something that has a permitted aspect, for wherever the prohibition is recognizable it has no nullification; and since one can remove the prohibition through hagalah it is as if recognizable — and he elaborated there that in these vessels the prohibition must impart taste in the permitted — see there where he elaborated; and Maharil in responsum siman 194 also disagrees with Rashba, for hagalah outlay is a small loss, as we reckon in chapter HaZahav (Bava Metzia daf 53) and in chapter 7 of Nedarim — maaser sheni has something that has a permitted aspect through bringing to Jerusalem, etc. — see there; and one should be concerned for his words where there is no great loss; and it appears one should wait until vessels are not ben yomo, then they are permitted from the Torah; and so at the end of responsa of Ben Lev — however he wrote there that this is only to use them all together, but alone not, since there is only taste it is not something that has a permitted aspect — see there; and in my humble opinion even each alone is forbidden to use until after twelve months, for Rashba and the Tur did not write it is not something that has a permitted aspect only because outlay is required; when there is Torah permission without outlay, all forbid. And Ben Lev ruled a dairy bowl mixed with meat bowls is not nullified in the majority since not in its kind — all forbidden; the Bach seized on this and was stringent to remove one vessel as mere stringency like Maharam with meat pieces — but all poskim agreed in siman 109 that one need not remove even a piece; Maharam did so because he was righteous and feared eating prohibition disgracefully, etc. — not applicable to vessels. Nevertheless, without loss, be stringent like the Bach; see siman 69 seif 10. The Taz also ruled one may kasher all vessels — see there.`,
    '4#א': `["That something that has a permitted aspect is not nullified, etc.; but its taste is nullified. And that if one whitened the food or filled it with chicken it is not nullified (in the first seif) — because the substance of the prohibition is considered present and it is not considered dissolved and not in existence except when not recognizable in appearance but only in taste — end of Torat Chatat there rule 2 in the name of Maharai; however it requires investigation also in this, for in my humble opinion from the Gemara Nedarim (daf 98) and Yerushalmi chapter 6 of Nedarim and Tosafot and Rosh and Ran in Nedarim there — something that has a permitted aspect, even its taste is not nullified in its kind — see there; and so is implied from what Maharam and Rosh wrote in responsum beginning general rule 2 on beer of new grain — the grain is nullified in sixty in water, and what we say that something that has a permitted aspect is not nullified refers to in its kind, etc.; and so is implied from Rambam, Semag, and Mechaber below siman 116 seif 10 regarding wine forbidden by vow mixed in other wine — even a drop in a barrel forbids all because one can ask about his vow; also what Torat Chatat wrote there that so is Taz Orach Chayim siman 513 in the name of Rabbeinu Tam is not necessary, for in my humble opinion the Tur there only said that in not its kind we do not say something that has a permitted aspect is not nullified, as implied in Beit Yosef and Bach there — see there; and this is what the Tur wrote here in the name of Rabbeinu Tam: that something that has a permitted aspect is not nullified specifically when mixed in its kind, but not in its kind taste is nullified in sixty — end of his words; thus in its kind even taste is not nullified; also what the Rav wrote in Torat Chatat that so is Orach Chayim general rule 25 — it is not so, for he ruled there in rule 4 like Rambam regarding wine above; and likewise regarding vessels absorbing prohibition he wrote there rule 19 like Rashba and Mechaber seif 3; and what he wrote there in rule 17 that where the substance of the prohibition is not in existence and there is only taste it is nullified — refers specifically to not its kind: "and that something that has a permitted aspect is not nullified refers specifically to in its kind; but in not its kind it is properly nullified in sixty even if one does not recognize it and the substance of the prohibition remains inside, etc.; and all the more so where the substance of the prohibition is not in existence and there is only taste it is properly nullified in sixty" — and so we learned in Yerushalmi that the Sages gave a measure for something that has a permitted aspect only in its kind in any amount and not in its kind in sixty — end of his words; thus it deals specifically with not its kind; and at first glance one could reconcile his words in the hagahah to say wherever the substance of the prohibition is still in the mixture, even if not in existence, such as wine in wine, etc., it is not nullified; but where a piece of prohibition fell and they threw it out whole — we hold one must nullify its emission as above siman 98 seif 8 on account of taste exiting from it — in such a case it is nullified; and now we can reconcile some proofs above dealing where the body of the item was mixed there and therefore not nullified; but from his words in Torat Chatat there it appears there is no distinction, for he brought so from Orach Chayim siman 513 dealing explicitly where the body of the item was mixed — see there; and so is implied from Maharai's words he brought there that I brought at the beginning of s.k. 7 — investigate; and he also brought this rule from Hagahot Alfasi chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah in the name of Mahariach — and there it states explicitly specifically where there is substance of the prohibition, but where there is no substance that dissolved it is nullified in sixty — end of his words; and so is implied in Darkei Moshe; and in truth for practical law it appears even if removed whole its taste is not nullified, as implied from all those proofs above; and so is implied from Rabeinu Yerucham part 4 that one who vowed from meat and it was cooked with meat — eggs are forbidden in sixty, for although it is something that has a permitted aspect, since it is not in its kind — end of his words; and otherwise it is strained to distinguish whether taste remains there or not — until you eat it in prohibition you eat it in permission. I found again in Bach who also ruled there is no distinction and it is never nullified, only he was brief — see there; and see in the next s.k.:`,
    '4#ב': `["And likewise if its prohibition is not on account of itself, etc. All seized on the Rav in this, for something that has a permitted aspect — what difference if absorbed or not, ultimately there is permission for its prohibition; and what he brought from Terumat HaDeshen siman 170 is on the contrary implied explicitly the opposite, as Maharshal wrote in Orach Chayim and in his book Yoreh Deah siman 88 — not so there, rather a piece worthy of honor: if the prohibition is not on account of itself but on account of prohibition absorbed in it, even though it is worthy of honor it is nullified, as poskim wrote at the beginning of siman 101; but regarding something that has a permitted aspect it is implied explicitly in Torat HaBayit that it is not nullified even if the prohibition is not on account of itself, for he considers there a piece that stood three days without salting as something that has a permitted aspect — if not because for roasting it was never forbidden — see there; and so in Hagahot ShaDa in the name of Maharai, and so in Be'er Sheva — see there; and on the contrary, since poskim wrote regarding a piece worthy of honor that absorbed a prohibition it is nullified because nevertheless the prohibition absorbed in it is not worthy of honor — it is implied that regarding something that has a permitted aspect, where the prohibition will have permission, it is not nullified."]`,
    '4#ג': `["Even though some say, etc. Meaning: and the main opinion is not so — it is not called something that has a permitted aspect since for roasting it was never forbidden, as written in siman 69 s.k. 56."]`,
    '4#ד': `["That was not recognizable, etc. Such as a vat full of grapes in clusters and there was much clear wine before Shabbat — permitted to take wine on Shabbat even though more constantly exits from the grapes, because it nullifies in what already exited and there is no something that has a permitted aspect since wine never had a name by itself; and likewise a barrel of whole grapes filled with water for tamad — grapes burst later and all wine exits — permitted to draw on Shabbat to drink, for the little wine exiting on Shabbat mixes and nullifies in wine already there that was never once recognizable alone — and similarly all such cases — there."]`,
    '4#ה': `["Such as chametz on Pesach, etc. And nevertheless some say in Orach Chayim siman 447 seif 9 that if chametz mixed dry on Pesach, even in a thousand it is not nullified; their reason as Mordechai and Rashba wrote: they did not say dry-with-dry is nullified except for prohibitions that forbid up to sixty, etc.; and that is why he wrote there seif 2 chametz mixed six hours and more does not forbid in any amount but its law is like other prohibitions; and if it were something that has a permitted aspect, even by taste also; and as Maggid Mishneh chapter 1 of Laws of Chametz in the name of Yerushalmi; however one can say in this that nevertheless it is nullified not in its kind like other prohibitions that have a permitted aspect; and what is not nullified on Pesach even not in its kind is on account of the severity of its prohibition, as Rambam chapter 15 of Forbidden Foods rule 12 wrote."]`,
    '4#ו': `["And there are those who disagree. And therefore what some say in Orach Chayim siman 447 there that chametz on Pesach dry-with-dry one against two is nullified — meaning not in its kind; for if in its kind it would be something that has a permitted aspect; and therefore it deals where there are sixty in the permitted pieces against the forbidden piece — otherwise even in other prohibitions it would not be nullified, since if cooked they impart taste, as below siman 109 s.k. 9; however one can say the two reasons the Mechaber brought in Orach Chayim also disagree whether chametz is something that has a permitted aspect; and there are those who disagree; and Torat Chatat there rule 4 ruled like the first reason, and Maharshal disagrees."]`,
    '4#ז': `["Such as one who cooks on Shabbat — intentionally, forbidden to him forever and permitted to others after Shabbat, as explained in Orach Chayim siman 318."]`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `Everything that has a permitted aspect. From the Torah one against two is nullified, as it is written "follow the majority"; and the Sages were stringent — since it has a permitted aspect later, one may not eat it in its prohibition through nullification. Rashi beginning of Beitzah.`,
    '1#ב': `Whether whole. Explanation: that otherwise it would not be nullified, for it is a counted item and is not nullified, as written in siman 86; and it teaches here that one should not err to think a whole egg has only the stringency of a counted item — it teaches there is also the stringency of something that has a permitted aspect; and there is a practical difference regarding doubt, as we will mention shortly. And in Perishah he elaborated on this in vain.`,
    '1#ג': `And even if there is doubt, etc. In this matter something that has a permitted aspect is stricter than other things that are not nullified — there we are lenient in their doubts if the prohibition is certainly d'rabbanan, we say in his doubt it is permitted like any d'rabbanan doubt and it will not harm him if it is an important item; but here, even in a d'rabbanan prohibition doubt, it is forbidden since it has a permitted aspect.`,
    '1#ד': `And it was mixed with forbidden others. And if that mixture was mixed again in a second mixture, he wrote below siman 110 seif 8 that it is permitted.`,
    '1#ה': `And if it was mixed not in its kind, etc. The reason I wrote above siman 101 seif 6 in the name of Torat Chatat: because the permission afterward is not called on the prohibition but on the mixture that was forbidden, and it is not the kind of the prohibition — and this reason is not sufficient enough. And I wonder why he did not write Ran's words in this chapter of one who vows from cooked food (Nedarim daf 52) — and this is the essence of his answer: we see according to Rabbi Yehudah who holds in its kind regarding prohibition and permission it is not nullified, and he derives this from "and take from the blood of the bull and from the blood of the goat" — even though the bull's blood is more than the goat's blood, it is called the goat's blood — thus it is not nullified because it is in its kind, for everything similar to its fellow does not weaken it, on the contrary it strengthens it; and the Sages do not derive this for prohibition and permission — for certainly bull and goat blood are both fit for sprinkling, unlike prohibition and permission — although they are one kind, nevertheless they differ, for this is forbidden and this permitted — therefore it is as not its kind; and therefore we say regarding something that has a permitted aspect it is comparable to bull and goat blood, for here there is no distinction between prohibition and permission, for the prohibition too will ultimately be permitted — and this is in its kind like bull and goat blood; unlike in its kind versus not its kind — although from the side of prohibition they are equal in something that has a permitted aspect, nevertheless there is a great distinction in that this is another kind — therefore they nullify one another. This is his intent there, even though it is not his language — and it is very correct.`,
    '1#ו': `However, if one whitened it, etc. He cited both: not only whitening food for appearance and taste, but even placing in a pot to fix — for that is also called its kind; everything one needs the other, like water and salt in dough, is its kind — so Tosafot end of chapter Meshilin; and what the Rama wrote that one filled it with chicken is because the substance of the prohibition is present — unlike what he wrote below that taste is not called something that has a permitted aspect. From this Maharai learned that everything placed to fix the pot is as its kind with the pot; and if one of them is something that has a permitted aspect it forbids the mixture up to a thousand; and one should not err from this regarding what we say in many places one against two is nullified from the Torah in its kind and in not its kind requires sixty from the Torah — to say there too it is its kind in what fixes the pot as here; for there the law depends on taste, since taste in not its kind, and this taste in the pot automatically has stringent name as not its kind.`,
    '2#_': `Will certainly come in any event. Explanation: such as an egg laid on Yom Tov that will certainly be permitted after Yom Tov — to exclude a doubtful tereifah egg cited afterward.`,
    '3#_': `A vessel that became forbidden, etc. It is puzzling why Rashba needed this reason that outlay is required that the Rama mentioned afterward — for the forbidden item, which is the vessel, cannot forbid more than the forbidding agent; and the forbidding agent absorbed in it would not have the law of something that has a permitted aspect, for that prohibition has no permission — if so the forbidden should not be stricter than the forbidding agent; and we find this reasoning many times in Tosafot regarding salt absorbed from blood that the Tur brings end of siman 105; and one can say that since it has a permitted aspect we should not heed any reasoning for leniency, for we may not rely on any leniency since it has permission anyway; and I already wrote in siman 69 seif 14 to learn another law from this regarding meat not salted; and this is what the Rama hinted here in what he wrote "and similarly all such cases," and Rashal Yoreh Deah siman 88 wrote another reason on the vessel that it is not something that has a permitted aspect since permission does not come automatically through time; nevertheless it appears that even though permitted to use all these vessels that were mixed, as Rashba wrote and Beit Yosef brought end of siman 122 — nevertheless one should not leave them a long time unless he kasher all from doubt, since the prohibition was nullified in the majority — it has the law of dry nullified in dry majority explained in siman 110, forbidden to eat them all together; and there is concern that in much time the mixture will be forgotten and one will come to cook a dish cooked in all the mixture together and eat them and come to prohibition — therefore he should kasher all from doubt, in my humble opinion; and see what is written in siman 122 on this law.`,
    '4#א': `But its taste is nullified. Explanation: if the prohibition was removed and only taste was emitted, that taste is not stringent to forbid on account of something that has a permitted aspect; and some say regarding what he wrote in seif 1 placing an egg in a pot to fix — there the egg is to give taste to the pot for the name of the dish — so Beit Yosef Orach Chayim siman 513.`,
    '4#ב': `Only on account of blood absorbed in it. This is written in Torat Chatat in the name of Terumat HaDeshen siman 170; Beit Yosef brought it in siman 69; and in truth he did not write this reason there to permit through absorption, but that it is not something that has a permitted aspect because permission is not for what was forbidden — namely cooking — and he concluded there that even a piece worthy of honor is nullified since here there is only absorbed prohibition — thus this reasoning of absorbed prohibition only serves to nullify a piece worthy of honor, not to nullify something that has a permitted aspect; for if so, why did Terumat HaDeshen write there for Rif it is something that has a permitted aspect since it has permission for roasting — even for Rif it is permitted because it was only absorbed prohibition? And further, why did the Rama need here a reason on vessels because of outlay and not say the reason was only absorbed prohibition? It appears the Rama wrote these matters according to Orach Chayim general rule 25 rule 19: "and a vessel forbidden through absorption of prohibition mixed with others is nullified in the majority, and even though one can kasher them it is not called something that has a permitted aspect to forbid all until kashered, since its prohibition is not from itself — no less than a whole beriah forbidden through absorption, and Beit Yosef explains in siman 122 in the name of Rashba because outlay is required," etc. — end of his words. Behold before you on the vessel law two reasons; therefore Rama first wrote on vessels Rashba's reason to learn a general rule that it is not called something that has a permitted aspect where outlay is required anywhere; afterward he wrote on an unsalted piece that this reason does not apply, and wrote another reason of Orach Chayim — on account of absorption; and although Torat Chatat wrote this in the name of Terumat HaDeshen siman 170 — this is because his Mishnah overpowered him and he erred in appearance of place, but in the substance of the law he wrote well according to Orach Chayim; and one may ask: what is different from a drop of wine forbidden by vow that fell in a barrel — that is something that has a permitted aspect, for there it was not absorption but mixture and the substance of the prohibition is there, and initially it was a separate entity — therefore the mixture does not permit it since it was something that has a permitted aspect; unlike this piece — but we are puzzled what reasoning exists that it should not be something that has a permitted aspect regarding absorbed prohibition: since in any event there is permission for this piece, they did not lighten to nullify it in the majority like everything that has a permitted aspect; granted regarding worthy of honor one can say they were stringent only if the prohibition is important, but not if the important piece is permitted and only has forbidden absorption — for nevertheless this piece has no prohibition from itself and prohibition does not apply on account of importance — thus prohibition alone and importance alone; but regarding something that has a permitted aspect it is primary: since it has permission without nullification we do not follow nullification — if so, everything that has permission for its prohibition from itself or for what is absorbed in it is equal; and one should not challenge: if so, it is also difficult on what he wrote that if there is no substance only taste it is not something that has a permitted aspect — for there it deals where one took the body of the prohibition that was not mixed in substance, only taste has no power to forbid, for taste was not sensed in its kind and it is as if the prohibition did not fall there at all — unlike here in an unsalted piece where prohibition is clear before us and even from the Torah requires nullification in majority — properly the Sages decreed nullification in majority does not help since it has permission anyway; and shortly we will mention more from this; and so Rashal on Torat Chatat's words above that regarding something that has a permitted aspect, that it is absorbed prohibition does not help. And further I am puzzled from the Mishnah in Bikkurim chapter 2 that said their growth is forbidden from eating in Jerusalem, etc. — meaning if bikkurim mixed in any amount, all is forbidden to eat in Jerusalem as ordinary produce, since it has permission to eat there as bikkurim; and if he planted the bikkurim in Jerusalem the growth is forbidden on account of something that has a permitted aspect — so Rambam explained this Mishnah, not as Rosh wrote to reject this explanation through Yerushalmi; and I wrote elsewhere it is not difficult on Rambam; and Bartenuro explained this planting refers to the mixture — even so the growth is forbidden on account of something that has a permitted aspect. If so we see even in this prohibition that is at least preferable to absorption in those growths — for even in growth of terumah that we learned chapter 9 of Terumot that is forbidden on account of a decree lest one keep impure terumah until planting time, and in bikkurim they said there on their growth that is ordinary — nevertheless we say growth of bikkurim mixture in Jerusalem, since there they have permission to eat them as bikkurim, we are stringent even in growth of bikkurim mixture — a fortiori we should be stringent on this unsalted piece that is forbidden by law to forbid the mixture on account of something that has a permitted aspect; and from this too the case we mentioned shortly is not difficult — if there is only taste of prohibition it is not something that has a permitted aspect, since one removed the body of the prohibition and only taste remains and it is in its kind — taste is not sensed at all initially and from the Torah no nullification is needed, for they did not say taste is like substance d'oraisa except in not its kind; and although from Tur Orach Chayim siman 513 where this law is in the name of Rabbeinu Tam it seems it deals with nosen taam — namely placing in food like whitening an egg in food — Beit Yosef already wrote this is not necessary; and from what I wrote the opposite is necessary — it deals only where kind mixed literally in kind and there is no taste at all; but here in bikkurim initially the body of bikkurim was among the mixture, and although nullified later in the ground when growth came from it — nevertheless permission is through nullification in this we say something that has a permitted aspect — no nullification helps; a fortiori in an unsalted piece that has full prohibition that you must nullify — this does not help for something that has a permitted aspect. From all this it appears clear to me: every prohibition in the world whose main permission is through nullification — permission of something that has a permitted aspect does not apply.`,
    '4#ג': `Every prohibition that was not recognizable. Such as grapes from which wine exits on Shabbat within clear wine that was there before Shabbat. This is not similar to an unsalted piece mentioned shortly, where the prohibition was known — for the piece was forbidden; unlike grapes where there was no prohibition at all initially.`,
    '4#ד': `One who vowed, etc. It is something that has a permitted aspect since he can ask a sage about the vow.`,
    '4#ה': `And there are those who disagree. This is Rambam who holds chametz on Pesach is something that has a permitted aspect; and Rashal chapter 25 siman 87 wrote that so appears to be the main view, not like Torat Chatat who ruled like Mordechai that it is the first opinion.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 102 seif 1 in the hagahah) However, if one whitened it in food or placed it in a pot to fix, it is not nullified. The reason poskim wrote: everything that needs one another is called for this purpose its kind, like water and salt in dough stated in the Gemara and poskim at the end of Beitzah (daf 39) and in Orach Chayim siman 513 — see there at length.`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 3) A vessel that became forbidden through absorption of prohibition and was mixed — it is nullified in the majority and we do not judge it like something that has a permitted aspect. This is specifically that it absorbed a prohibition that has no permitted aspect — it is not like something that has a permitted aspect what becomes permitted through hagalah, because one must expend outlay, etc.; but a vessel that absorbed a prohibition that has a permitted aspect — see Shach and poskim.`,
    '3#_': `(Seif 4 in the hagahah) One who vowed from one matter and it was mixed afterward. See in Peri Megadim in the introduction to Laws of Yom Tov who wrote: one who vowed benefit and it was mixed — one can say it is nullified, for when we say something that has a permitted aspect is not nullified, that is for eating, not for benefit; and likewise for moving one can say it is nullified — see there; and so Tzelach: granted for eating and benefit, but for moving, etc. — see there.`,
    '4#_': `(There in the hagahah) If it remains forbidden for one forever even though permitted for others, such as one who cooks on Shabbat — it is not called something that has a permitted aspect. And see Magen Avraham in Orach Chayim siman 318 — it deals with mixing after Shabbat; for if on Shabbat it is literally something that has a permitted aspect after Shabbat relative to others; and similarly regarding what is written in siman 318 — see there.`,
  },
};
